    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/humbert-i-count-of-savoy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/humbert-i-count-of-savoy.jpg" alt="Humbert I, Count of Savoy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/humbert-i-count-of-savoy">Humbert I, Count of Savoy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comte_Humbert_I_de_Savoie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pelagio Palagi, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The house of Savoy had ruled lands on both sides of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> since the eleventh century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humbert-i-count-of-savoy">Humbert the White-Handed</a> held counties around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake of Geneva</a> and the valleys leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-cenis">Mont Cenis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-st-bernard-pass">Great St Bernard</a> passes. By the early fifteenth century the County of Savoy stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bresse">Bresse</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a> in the north-west to the coast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, which had placed itself under Savoyard protection in 1388. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-viii-duke-of-savoy">Amadeus VIII</a> bought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-of-geneva">County of Geneva</a>, the Genevois, in 1401, and on 19 February 1416 Emperor Sigismund, visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a>, raised him to the rank of duke, making Savoy an imperial duchy. Two years later the extinction of the Savoy-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> branch brought the principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, back under the main line. Amadeus VIII thus created a state that combined French-speaking lands in Savoy, Bresse and the Vaud with Italian-speaking Piedmont. In 1434 he retired to his priory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chateau-de-ripaille">Ripaille</a> on the Lake of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, and in 1439 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-basel">Council of Basel</a> elected him antipope as Felix V, a dignity he gave up in 1449. His reign left a larger, more coherent duchy, but its position between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Swiss and the powers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> would expose it to repeated invasion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chambery"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chambery.jpg" alt="Chambéry" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chamb%C3%A9ry_-_Place_St-L%C3%A9ger.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Florian Pépellin, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The duchy was poor in comparison with its neighbours. Savoy proper was a land of mountain pastures, forests and small towns, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a> as its seat of government; the population lived largely from livestock, dairying and seasonal migration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, more fertile and populous, produced grain, wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and later raw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, which became its main export in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, sent to the looms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Tolls on the Alpine passes and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a> brought steady revenue, and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> and nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/villefranche-sur-mer">Villefranche</a> gave a modest outlet to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Society was dominated by a landed nobility, many of whom served the dukes as officers and officials, alongside a clergy loyal to the Catholic Church. The duchy acquired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shroud-of-turin">Shroud of Turin</a> in 1453, and the relic, moved from Chambéry to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> in 1578, became a focus of dynastic devotion, housed from 1694 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guarino-guarini">Guarino Guarini</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapel-of-the-holy-shroud">Chapel of the Holy Shroud</a>. Protestant Waldensian communities in the valleys west of Turin were repeatedly persecuted, most notoriously in the massacres of 1655 known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmontese-easter">Piedmontese Easter</a> and in the expulsion of 1686, before they returned in 1689 and won toleration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saint-quentin-1557"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-saint-quentin-1557.jpg" alt="Battle of Saint-Quentin (1557)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saint-quentin-1557">Battle of Saint-Quentin (1557)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emanuele_Filiberto_vince_i_Francesi_a_San_Quintino.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Massimo d'Azeglio, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Weak in the fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries, Savoy suffered its worst disaster in 1536, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-duke-of-nevers">Francis I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> occupied Savoy and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, Bern conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> established its independence under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-prince-of-monaco">Charles III</a> was left with a few fortresses. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-philibert-duke-of-savoy">Emmanuel Philibert</a>, serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>, commanded the Spanish army that crushed the French at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saint-quentin-1557">Battle of Saint-Quentin</a> in 1557, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-cateau-cambresis">Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis</a> in 1559 restored most of his lands. He built a militia and the pentagonal citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-emmanuel-i-duke-of-savoy">Charles Emmanuel</a> I seized the Marquisate of Saluzzo in 1588 and kept it by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lyon-1601">Treaty of Lyon</a> in 1601, ceding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bresse">Bresse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugey">Bugey</a> to France, but his night assault on Geneva, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/l-escalade">Escalade</a> of 1602, failed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> fought both sides in the wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>: defeated by the French at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-staffarda">Staffarda</a> in 1690 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marsaglia">Marsaglia</a> in 1693, he made a separate peace in 1696 that returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinerolo">Pinerolo</a>, and in 1706 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a> broke the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-turin">Siege of Turin</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-emmanuel-iii-of-sardinia">Charles Emmanuel III</a> the Savoyard army defended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, winning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-assietta">Battle of the Assietta</a> in 1747.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-viii-duke-of-savoy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amadeus-viii-duke-of-savoy.jpg" alt="Amadeus VIII, Duke of Savoy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-viii-duke-of-savoy">Amadeus VIII, Duke of Savoy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amadeus_VIII_of_Savoy_from_a_French_edition_of_Albertanus_of_Brescia%27s_De_doctrina_dicendi_et_tacendi_and_De_amore_et_dilectione_Dei_et_proximi_et_aliarum_rerum_et_de_forma_vitae.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-viii-duke-of-savoy">Amadeus VIII</a> gave the duchy one of the first general law codes of a European state, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statuta-sabaudiae">Statuta Sabaudiae</a></em> of 1430, and ruled through a council and an itinerant court. Representative estates met for Savoy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> but lost importance after the sixteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-philibert-duke-of-savoy">Emmanuel Philibert</a>, restored in 1559, laid the foundations of an absolute state: he moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> in 1563, made Italian the language of administration in Piedmont and French in Savoy, ceased summoning the estates and built up a permanent army and tax system. The state was nonetheless shaken by regencies, above all the civil war of 1639–1642 between the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christine-of-france">Christine of France</a> and her brothers-in-law. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> (r. 1675–1730) completed the absolutist programme. He carried out a general land survey to tax noble and church land, reformed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-turin">University of Turin</a>, reduced feudal privileges and issued the Royal Constitutions of 1723 and 1729, a consolidated code. From 1713 the dukes of Savoy were also kings, first of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and from 1720 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, but the mainland duchy, Piedmont and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> remained the heart of the composite Savoyard state, each with its own law and institutions until the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-albert-of-sardinia">Charles Albert</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-superga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basilica-of-superga.jpg" alt="Basilica of Superga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-superga">Basilica of Superga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilica_di_Superga_(Turin).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paris Orlando, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Duchy of Savoy is best known as the seed of the Italian monarchy. From an Alpine duchy its rulers built, by marriage, war and diplomacy, the state that unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the nineteenth century: the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, enlarged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> in 1815, led the wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and in 1861 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a> became the first King of Italy. The house of Savoy reigned there until 1946. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, chosen as capital in 1563, was rebuilt as a planned baroque city with arcaded streets and squares; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guarino-guarini">Guarino Guarini</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-juvarra">Filippo Juvarra</a>, who designed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-superga">Basilica of Superga</a>, the hunting lodge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palazzina-di-caccia-of-stupinigi">Stupinigi</a> and the façade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palazzo-madama-turin">Palazzo Madama</a>, made it one of the architectural capitals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the Savoy residences are now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Savoyard reforms under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a>, especially the cadastre and the codification of law, were studied by other eighteenth-century rulers. On the French side, the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a> in 1536 shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and Savoy proper, French in language, eventually joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shroud-of-turin">Shroud of Turin</a>, owned by the house of Savoy until 1983, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-saints-maurice-and-lazarus">Order of Saints Maurice and Lazarus</a> remain legacies of the dynasty.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars.jpg" alt="French Revolutionary Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_Revolutionary_Wars.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ruedi33a, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The duchy was absorbed into the larger state its rulers created. After 1720 the Savoyard sovereigns took their highest title from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> remained their capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> broke this composite state apart: French troops overran Savoy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> in 1792, and Savoy was annexed as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-blanc-department">Mont-Blanc</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-cherasco">Armistice of Cherasco</a> in 1796 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-iii-of-sardinia">Victor Amadeus III</a> ceded both territories, and in December 1798 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-emmanuel-iv-of-sardinia">Charles Emmanuel IV</a> was forced to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and withdraw to the island of Sardinia; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed Piedmont in 1802. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> restored the mainland territories in 1814–1815 and added the former Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. On 29 November 1847 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-albert-of-sardinia">Charles Albert</a> decreed the ‘perfect fusion’, which abolished the separate administrations of Sardinia and the mainland states and created a single kingdom with common institutions, ending the Duchy of Savoy as a distinct state; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statuto-albertino">Statuto Albertino</a> of 1848 then gave the kingdom a constitution. In 1860, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turin-1860">Treaty of Turin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-ii">Victor Emmanuel II</a> and Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a> ceded Savoy and Nice to France in return for French support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a>, and the ancestral duchy became the French departments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savoie">Savoie</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haute-savoie">Haute-Savoie</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Duchy of Savoy turned an Alpine dynasty astride <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> into an absolutist military state, rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> as a baroque capital and won royal crowns, creating the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> that led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> while its own ancestral lands finally passed to France in 1860.</em></p>

